NoteLearning objectives



By the end of this lecture you will be able to:

write the simple linear regression model and state its LINE assumptions;
fit the model in R and interpret the intercept and slope in context;
partition variability with ANOVA and interpret the MSE and \(R^2\);
test the slope with a t-test and the overall model with an F-test;
tell a confidence interval for the mean response apart from a prediction interval;
check the assumptions with residual diagnostics and influence measures.

NoteLearning objectives



By the end of this lecture you will be able to:

classify a variable as nominal, ordinal, discrete, or continuous, and explain why the type decides the method;
choose and compute measures of center and spread for symmetric and skewed data;
compute probabilities from the normal, binomial, and Poisson distributions in R;
explain the difference between a standard deviation and a standard error;
interpret a 95% confidence interval and the result of a hypothesis test correctly;
run and read one-sample, two-sample, and paired t-tests.

NoteLearning objectives



By the end of this lecture you will be able to:

match the type of outcome (continuous, binary, count, time to event) to a regression model;
explain why logistic regression, not linear regression, is used for a binary outcome;
interpret odds ratios from simple and adjusted logistic models;
describe how adjustment addresses confounding and how dummy variables code a categorical predictor;
test an interaction (effect modification) between two predictors;
check multicollinearity and influence, and compare nested models with AIC, BIC, and likelihood ratio tests.
